  {
   "id": "S12",
   "act": "ACT6",
   "title": "Who actually wins?",
   "lead_in": 0.4,
   "tail": 0.4,
   "vo": [
    {
     "id": "L110",
     "text": "So who actually wins?",
     "pause": 0.6
    },
    {
     "id": "L111",
     "text": "The chip designers capture the richest margins today, but they depend on a handful of buyers. Earlier this year, three direct customers accounted for more than half of Nvidia's revenue.",
     "pause": 0.35
    },
    {
     "id": "L112",
     "text": "The foundries and toolmakers hold positions that are almost impossible to copy, but they must keep investing tens of billions just to stay ahead.",
     "pause": 0.35
    },
    {
     "id": "L113",
     "text": "The cloud giants carry the biggest bill of all, and investors are watching how much of that spending turns into profit.",
     "pause": 0.35
    },
    {
     "id": "L114",
     "text": "The grid's equipment makers have something rarer: order books that stretch for years, from customers who cannot wait.",
     "pause": 0.4
    },
    {
     "id": "L115",
     "text": "And someone has to pay. On America's largest grid, run by PJM, the price of capacity has jumped more than tenfold in three years. Its independent market monitor attributes more than twenty-three billion dollars of added capacity costs to data centre demand. Costs that are passed on to electricity customers.",
     "pause": 0.45
    },
    {
     "id": "L116",
     "text": "None of this guarantees the future. Backlogs can shrink. Forecasts can be wrong. And capacity built for a boom can sit idle in a downturn.",
     "pause": 0.7
    }
   ],
